{"id": "4eb22963-46c2-484e-8d35-9eda7bd0f6a8", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T23:24:57.758449", "source": "user", "parent_id": "ab0290a6-6a11-47fd-9d03-515229cddc21", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Your proposed experimental approach is sound and aligns well with the design-only brief. You may proceed with Option A (the linear-cycle hybrid formulation with learnable cycle representations), while specifying how multi-periodic components (e.g., daily and weekly harmonics) can be incorporated.\\n\\nPlease develop the complete, end-to-end experimental design specification satisfying the submission contract. Ensure your deliverable includes:\\n1. Precise mathematical formulation of CycleNet (cycle indexing, representation, trend/residual handling, and normalization/denormalization).\\n2. Implementation and training specification (loss functions, optimization setup, learning rate scheduling, regularization, and computational complexity).\\n3. Experimental setup, datasets (real and synthetic), baselines, and evaluation controls.\\n4. Comprehensive analysis and ablation plan (evaluating periodic sensitivity, embedding capacity, and decomposition benefits).\\n5. Feasibility and practical deployment considerations.\\n\\nRemember not to claim that experiments were executed or report fabricated empirical test numbers.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
{"id": "297ab661-6e3f-453a-9e40-ed2b618b9db8", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T23:25:51.761784", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "4eb22963-46c2-484e-8d35-9eda7bd0f6a8", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experimental Design Specification: Implementation, Training, and Evaluation of CycleNet for Periodic Time Series Forecasting\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Problem Scope\n\nPeriodic time series forecasting is critical across diverse domains—including power grid load forecasting, traffic flow optimization, and environmental monitoring. Conventional deep architectures (e.g., vanilla Transformers) often fail to maintain strict phase consistency across long horizons or incur excessive computational overhead, whereas simple linear models (e.g., DLinear) may lack explicit capacity to represent recurring intra-cycle dynamics and multi-harmonic patterns.\n\n**CycleNet** bridges this gap through a **linear-cycle hybrid architecture**. By coupling a learnable periodic cycle representation with linear projection backbones and trend-residual decoupling, CycleNet explicitly incorporates periodic priors while preserving computational efficiency ($O(L \\cdot H)$ linear parameter complexity per channel). This document provides an end-to-end, reproducible experimental design to implement, train, and comprehensively benchmark CycleNet against modern forecasting paradigms.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mathematical Formulation of CycleNet\n\n### 2.1. Notation and Input/Output Spaces\nLet $\\mathbf{X}_{1:L} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{L \\times C}$ denote the historical lookback window of length $L$ across $C$ variates, where $\\mathbf{x}_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^C$ is the observation vector at discrete time index $t \\in \\{1, \\dots, L\\}$. The objective is to forecast the future values $\\mathbf{Y}_{L+1:L+H} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H \\times C}$ over a forecasting horizon $H$.\n\n### 2.2. Reversible Instance Normalization (RevIN)\nTo handle non-stationary distribution shifts (mean drift and variance scaling across temporal windows), CycleNet applies Reversible Instance Normalization without affine parameters (or with learnable affine scaling):\n\n$$\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_X = \\frac{1}{L} \\sum_{t=1}^L \\mathbf{x}_t \\in \\mathbb{R}^C, \\quad \\boldsymbol{\\sigma}_X^2 = \\frac{1}{L} \\sum_{t=1}^L (\\mathbf{x}_t - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_X)^2 + \\epsilon \\cdot \\mathbf{1} \\in \\mathbb{R}^C$$\n\nThe normalized input $\\tilde{\\mathbf{X}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{L \\times C}$ is computed as:\n$$\\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}_t = \\frac{\\mathbf{x}_t - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_X}{\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}_X}, \\quad \\forall t \\in \\{1, \\dots, L\\}$$\n\nUpon generating the normalized prediction $\\tilde{\\mathbf{Y}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H \\times C}$, the final forecast is retrieved via the exact inverse operation:\n$$\\hat{\\mathbf{y}}_{L+h} = \\tilde{\\mathbf{y}}_{L+h} \\odot \\boldsymbol{\\sigma}_X + \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_X, \\quad \\forall h \\in \\{1, \\dots, H\\}$$\n\n### 2.3. Cycle Indexing and Learnable Cycle Representations\n\n#### Single Dominant Period\nLet $S \\in \\mathbb{N}^+$ be the fundamental period length (e.g., $S = 24$ for hourly observations with daily seasonality). Every absolute time step $t$ corresponds to an intra-cycle phase index $p_t \\in \\{0, 1, \\dots, S-1\\}$:\n$$p_t = t \\pmod S$$\n\nCycleNet instantiates a learnable cycle memory matrix $\\mathbf{C} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{S \\times D}$, where $D$ is the embedding dimension. For channel-independent architectures, $\\mathbf{C}$ can be shared across all variates or instantiated per channel $\\mathbf{C}^{(c)} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{S \\times D}$ ($c \\in \\{1, \\dots, C\\}$).\n\nFor the lookback window and the future horizon, the cycle features are sliced by phase:\n$$\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{in}} = \\left[ \\mathbf{C}[p_1], \\mathbf{C}[p_2], \\dots, \\mathbf{C}[p_L] \\right]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^{L \\times D}$$\n$$\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{out}} = \\left[ \\mathbf{C}[p_{L+1}], \\mathbf{C}[p_{L+2}], \\dots, \\mathbf{C}[p_{L+H}] \\right]^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H \\times D}$$\n\n#### Multi-Periodic Extension (Harmonic Superposition)\nReal-world systems frequently feature multiple overlapping seasonalities (e.g., daily cycle $S_1 = 24$ and weekly cycle $S_2 = 168$). CycleNet extends the learnable cycle representation via multi-harmonic additive superposition:\n$$\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{multi}}[t] = \\sum_{m=1}^M \\mathbf{W}_m \\mathbf{C}^{(m)}\\left[ t \\pmod{S_m} \\right]$$\nwhere $M$ is the number of identified periodicities, $\\mathbf{C}^{(m)} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{S_m \\times D_m}$ is the cycle dictionary for the $m$-th frequency component, and $\\mathbf{W}_m \\in \\mathbb{R}^{D \\times D_m}$ is a linear projection aligning channel capacities.\n\n### 2.4. Decoupled Architecture: Cycle & Trend/Residual Branches\n\nThe normalized input $\\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}$ is decomposed into a trend/slowly varying residual component and a cyclical component:\n\n1. **Trend/Residual Branch**:\n   The trend/residual sequence captures long-term non-periodic evolutions. It is extracted either via a 1D average pooling kernel of size $k$ (moving average filter, $k \\approx S$) or directly modeled via a linear residual backbone:\n   $$\\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}_{\\text{trend}} = \\text{AvgPool1D}(\\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}, \\text{kernel\\_size}=k, \\text{stride}=1, \\text{padding}=\\lfloor k/2 \\rfloor)$$\n   $$\\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}_{\\text{res}} = \\tilde{\\mathbf{X}} - \\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}_{\\text{trend}}$$\n   The trend forecast is mapped linearly across the temporal dimension:\n   $$\\tilde{\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\text{trend}} = \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{trend}} \\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}_{\\text{trend}} + \\mathbf{b}_{\\text{trend}}, \\quad \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{trend}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H \\times L}$$\n\n2. **Cycle Branch**:\n   The cyclical dynamics are captured by combining historical cyclical deviations $\\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}_{\\text{res}}$ with the learnable future cycle prototypes $\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{out}}$:\n   $$\\tilde{\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\text{cycle}} = \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hist}} \\tilde{\\mathbf{X}}_{\\text{res}} + \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{proj}} \\mathbf{C}_{\\text{out}}$$\n   where $\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hist}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{H \\times L}$ maps historical cyclical residuals into the forecast horizon, and $\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{proj}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{1 \\times D}$ projects the multidimensional future cycle embedding into univariate channel values (or $\\mathbb{R}^{C \\times D}$ for channel-dependent modeling).\n\n3. **Composite Aggregation**:\n   The complete normalized forecast combines the outputs of both branches:\n   $$\\tilde{\\mathbf{Y}} = \\tilde{\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\text{trend}} + \\tilde{\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\text{cycle}}$$\n   followed by the RevIN denormalization step to produce the final physical prediction $\\hat{\\mathbf{Y}}$.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Implementation and Training Specification\n\n### 3.1. Loss Function Formulations\n1. **Primary Forecasting Loss (Mean Squared Error - MSE)**:\n   $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{MSE}} = \\frac{1}{H \\cdot C} \\sum_{h=1}^H \\sum_{c=1}^C \\left( Y_{L+h, c} - \\hat{Y}_{L+h, c} \\right)^2$$\n\n2. **Periodic Smoothness Regularization (Optional Auxiliary Loss)**:\n   To prevent rapid, unphysical high-frequency fluctuations within the learnable cycle embedding table $\\mathbf{C} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{S \\times D}$, a circular Dirichlet / Total Variation penalty is imposed:\n   $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{reg}}(\\mathbf{C}) = \\frac{1}{S} \\sum_{s=0}^{S-1} \\left\\| \\mathbf{C}[(s+1) \\pmod S] - \\mathbf{C}[s] \\right\\|_2^2$$\n\n3. **Composite Objective**:\n   $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{total}} = \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{MSE}} + \\lambda_{\\text{reg}} \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{reg}}(\\mathbf{C}), \\quad \\text{with } \\lambda_{\\text{reg}} \\in \\{0, 10^{-4}, 10^{-3}\\}$$\n\n### 3.2. Optimization Setup & Hyperparameters\n- **Optimizer**: AdamW ($\\beta_1 = 0.9, \\beta_2 = 0.999$, weight decay $= 10^{-4}$).\n- **Learning Rate Schedule**: Cosine Annealing learning rate schedule with an initial learning rate $\\eta_{\\text{init}} = 10^{-3}$ and minimum learning rate $\\eta_{\\text{min}} = 10^{-6}$ over $E = 50$ epochs, preceded by a linear warm-up phase of 5 epochs.\n- **Batch Size**: 32 for multivariate benchmarks ($C > 20$); 64 or 128 for lower-dimensional series ($C \\le 7$).\n- **Early Stopping**: Patience of 10 epochs monitoring validation MSE loss; best model weights are checkpointed and reloaded for testing.\n- **Gradient Clipping**: Maximal $\\ell_2$-norm threshold of $1.0$ to ensure numerical stability during initial training iterations.\n\n### 3.3. Channel Strategy: Channel Independence (CI) vs. Channel Mixing (CM)\n- CycleNet adopts a **Channel-Independent (CI)** paradigm by default: weights $(\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{trend}}, \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{hist}})$ are shared across all $C$ channels while the cycle representation $\\mathbf{C}$ can be shared globally across channels to induce strong inductive bias and minimize parameter footprint.\n- For high-dimensional datasets ($C > 100$), CI prevents overfitting and drastically reduces training latency.\n\n### 3.4. Computational Complexity and Memory Footprint\n- **Time Complexity per Batch**:\n  - RevIN: $O(L \\cdot C)$\n  - Moving Average Decomposition: $O(L \\cdot C)$\n  - Linear Projections: $O(L \\cdot H \\cdot C)$\n  - Cycle Lookup and Projection: $O(H \\cdot D \\cdot C)$\n  - Total time complexity is strictly linear in input/output lengths: $\\mathcal{O}\\left(C \\cdot (L \\cdot H + H \\cdot D)\\right)$, avoiding the quadratic $\\mathcal{O}(L^2)$ attention bottleneck of standard Transformers.\n- **Parameter Footprint**:\n  - Total parameters: $P \\approx 2 \\cdot (L \\times H) + (S \\times D) + (D \\times 1)$.\n  - For $L=96, H=96, S=24, D=16$: $P \\approx 2(9216) + 384 + 16 \\approx 18.8\\text{k}$ parameters. This allows real-time CPU deployment and ultra-fast training iterations.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Experimental Setup, Datasets, and Evaluation Controls\n\n### 4.1. Benchmark Datasets\n\n#### 1. Real-World Benchmarks\nAll datasets are partitioned chronologically (70% Train, 10% Validation, 20% Test) with strict avoidance of lookahead leakage:\n- **Electricity Load (ECL)**: Hourly electricity consumption of 321 clients. Fundamental cycle $S = 24$ (daily) and $S = 168$ (weekly).\n- **Traffic (PeMS)**: Hourly road occupancy rates from California Department of Transportation (862 sensors). Strong daily commute cycle ($S = 24$).\n- **Weather**: Local climatological data recorded at 10-minute intervals (21 variables). Diurnal cycle $S = 144$ (steps per 24 hours).\n- **ETT (Electricity Transformer Temperature)**:\n  - Hourly series: $\\text{ETTh1}, \\text{ETTh2}$ ($S = 24$).\n  - 15-minute series: $\\text{ETTm1}, \\text{ETTm2}$ ($S = 96$).\n\n#### 2. Synthetic Benchmark (Controlled Periodic Stress-Testing)\nTo evaluate CycleNet under isolated, controllable conditions, we define a synthetic data generation process with known ground-truth periodicity:\n$$x_t = \\sum_{m=1}^2 A_m \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{S_m} + \\phi_m\\right) + \\beta \\cdot t + \\gamma_t + \\epsilon_t$$\nwhere:\n- Primary periods: $S_1 = 24$ (daily), $S_2 = 168$ (weekly).\n- $\\beta \\cdot t$: Linear trend component ($\\beta \\in \\{0.0, 0.05\\}$).\n- $\\gamma_t$: Abrupt regime-shift mean jump at $t = T/2$.\n- $\\epsilon_t \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\sigma^2)$: Gaussian noise with controlled signal-to-noise ratios (SNR $\\in \\{5\\text{dB}, 15\\text{dB}, 30\\text{dB}\\}$).\n\n### 4.2. Baseline Models\nCycleNet is evaluated against competitive models spanning diverse design paradigms:\n1. **Linear/Decomposition Baselines**:\n   - **DLinear**: Direct linear decomposition mapping seasonal and trend components.\n   - **NLinear**: Linear model with normalization subtraction to handle non-stationarity.\n   - **TiDE**: Time-series Dense Encoder-decoder incorporating static and dynamic covariates.\n2. **2D & Multi-Periodic Neural Models**:\n   - **TimesNet**: Transforms 1D series into 2D variations based on dominant FFT frequencies and applies 2D convolutions.\n3. **Patch-based & Attention Transformers**:\n   - **PatchTST**: State-of-the-art patch-based Transformer with channel independence.\n   - **Autoformer**: Auto-correlation mechanism with built-in seasonal-trend decomposition.\n\n### 4.3. Evaluation Controls & Experimental Protocol\n- **Fixed Lookback Window**: Standardized input length $L = 96$ (and $L = 336$ for long-history analysis).\n- **Forecasting Horizons**: $H \\in \\{96, 192, 336, 720\\}$ steps across all datasets.\n- **Statistical Significance**: Each experimental run is executed across **3 independent random initialization seeds** (e.g., seeds `[2024, 2025, 2026]`). Results are reported as mean $\\pm$ standard error.\n- **Evaluation Metrics**:\n  - **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**: $\\frac{1}{N H C} \\sum_{i=1}^N \\sum_{h=1}^H \\sum_{c=1}^C (Y_{h, c}^{(i)} - \\hat{Y}_{h, c}^{(i)})^2$\n  - **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)**: $\\frac{1}{N H C} \\sum_{i=1}^N \\sum_{h=1}^H \\sum_{c=1}^C |Y_{h, c}^{(i)} - \\hat{Y}_{h, c}^{(i)}|$\n  - **Computational Efficiency**: Number of trainable parameters, Multiply-Accumulate operations (MACs), and inference latency (milliseconds per sample on CPU and GPU).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Comprehensive Analysis and Ablation Plan\n\nTo isolate the source of predictive gains and test robustness, the experiment design incorporates five structured ablation experiments:\n\n### Ablation 1: Efficacy of Learnable Cycle Representations\n- **Hypothesis**: Learnable cycle embeddings $\\mathbf{C} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{S \\times D}$ outperform static mathematical encodings by adapting to asymmetric, non-sinusoidal waveforms.\n- **Configurations Compared**:\n  1. *CycleNet (Full)*: Learnable cycle embedding dictionary $\\mathbf{C}$.\n  2. *CycleNet-Fourier*: Fixed sinusoidal harmonics: $\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{fixed}}[t, 2k] = \\sin(2\\pi k t / S)$, $\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{fixed}}[t, 2k+1] = \\cos(2\\pi k t / S)$.\n  3. *CycleNet-ZeroCycle*: $\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{out}}$ disabled (reduces to a standard DLinear/linear mapping).\n  4. *CycleNet-OneHot*: Static one-hot phase encoding.\n\n### Ablation 2: Robustness to Period Misspecification & Phase Jitter\n- **Hypothesis**: In real-world data, the exact period may exhibit drift or measurement inaccuracies.\n- **Test Protocol**: On the synthetic dataset and Electricity dataset:\n  - Perturb the specified period $S_{\\text{spec}} \\in \\{S - 2, S - 1, S, S + 1, S + 2\\}$.\n  - Inject random phase shifts / timestamp jitter $\\Delta t \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(p)$ to assess CycleNet's degradation curve under imperfect temporal alignment.\n\n### Ablation 3: Decomposition Mechanics (Cycle + Trend vs. Monolithic)\n- **Hypothesis**: Explicit separation of trend/residuals prevents non-periodic drifts from corrupting the periodic representation.\n- **Configurations Compared**:\n  1. *Dual-branch CycleNet* (Trend + Cycle branches).\n  2. *Single-branch CycleNet* (Direct mapping: $\\hat{\\mathbf{Y}} = \\mathbf{W} \\tilde{\\mathbf{X}} + \\mathbf{W}_c \\mathbf{C}_{\\text{out}}$ without residual decoupling).\n  3. *Cycle-only*: Omitting the trend branch entirely.\n\n### Ablation 4: Multi-Periodic Superposition Analysis\n- **Hypothesis**: Modeling compound cycles (e.g., daily + weekly) simultaneously yields lower long-term error ($H = 720$) than relying on a single base period.\n- **Configurations Compared**:\n  - Single cycle: $S_1 = 24$ (diurnal only).\n  - Single cycle: $S_2 = 168$ (weekly only).\n  - Harmonic Superposition: Multi-harmonic additive cycle modeling ($S_1 = 24 + S_2 = 168$).\n\n### Ablation 5: Cycle Representation Capacity Scaling\n- **Hypothesis**: There is an optimal latent dimension $D$ balancing expressive capacity against parameter redundancy.\n- **Test Grid**: Sweep embedding dimension $D \\in \\{1, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64\\}$. Evaluate both predictive accuracy (MSE) and parameter efficiency.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Feasibility, Practical Deployment, and Reproducibility\n\n### 6.1. Deployment Considerations\n1. **Low-Latency Edge Inference**:\n   Because the cycle representation $\\mathbf{C}_{\\text{out}}$ for future horizons depends solely on the time index $t \\pmod S$, it can be pre-computed and stored as a static lookup table in edge deployments. The remaining operations are single matrix multiplications and vector additions, enabling sub-millisecond inference on modest CPU architectures.\n2. **Handling Missing Observations & Phase Alignment**:\n   In streaming environments with missing data points, naive index increments can cause phase shift errors. The deployment pipeline must rely on calendar-based absolute timestamps (modulo $S$ over elapsed time) rather than sequential sample counts, ensuring phase synchronization is invariant to missing observations.\n3. **Cold-Start Adaptation**:\n   When deploying to new time series channels with minimal history, the shared cycle table $\\mathbf{C}$ serves as a strong global periodic prior, stabilizing predictions even when historical lookback windows are noisy.\n\n### 6.2. Sanity Checks and Dry-Run Assertions\nTo guarantee bug-free implementation before executing full benchmarks, the following programmatic sanity assertions should be embedded in the test pipeline:\n- **Phase Invariance**: Verifying that a circular roll of input and timestamp indices by exactly $k \\cdot S$ produces an identically rolled forecast.\n- **Reversible Normalization Invertibility**: Asserting $\\|\\text{RevIN}^{-1}(\\text{RevIN}(\\mathbf{X})) - \\mathbf{X}\\|_\\infty < 10^{-6}$.\n- **Gradient Flow Check**: Ensuring non-zero gradients flow to both the linear trend weights $\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{trend}}$ and the cycle table $\\mathbf{C}$ after the backward pass:\n  $$\\min(\\|\\nabla_{\\mathbf{W}_{\\text{trend}}} \\mathcal{L}\\|_2, \\|\\nabla_{\\mathbf{C}} \\mathcal{L}\\|_2) > 0$$\n\n---\n\n## 7. Summary of Complete Experimental Workflow\n\n```\n[Time Series Data] \n       │\n       ▼\n[Data Preprocessing & Chronological Splitting (70/10/20)]\n       │\n       ▼\n[Periodicity Identification (FFT / Sampling Rate S1, S2)]\n       │\n       ▼\n[CycleNet Architecture]\n  ├── RevIN Normalization\n  ├── Trend/Residual Decomposition (Moving Average)\n  ├── Dual Branch Processing:\n  │     ├── Trend Branch: W_trend * X_trend\n  │     └── Cycle Branch: W_hist * X_res + W_proj * C_out(t mod S)\n  ├── Composite Sum: Y_norm = Y_trend + Y_cycle\n  └── RevIN Denormalization -> Final Predictions\n       │\n       ▼\n[Optimization (AdamW, Cosine Annealing, Early Stopping on Val MSE)]\n       │\n       ▼\n[Evaluation against Baselines (DLinear, PatchTST, TimesNet, TiDE)]\n       │\n       ▼\n[Ablations: Learnable vs. Static Cycles, S Misspecification, Capacity D]\n```\n\nThis experimental design provides a self-contained, mathematically explicit, and methodologically sound specification to implement, train, and validate CycleNet for periodic time series forecasting without relying on unverified assumptions or fabricated outcomes.", "evidence": "Completed exploratory checks on the container environment (/workspace, Python sys.path, and pip list) confirming that no pre-existing code or dependencies are present, and validated that the design adheres to Option A (linear-cycle hybrid with multi-periodic harmonic extensions) as instructed and approved by the researcher.", "assumptions": ["Task requirements dictate a design-only specification without physical model execution or fabrication of empirical numerical results.", "The standard public time series benchmarks (Electricity, Traffic, Weather, ETT) follow their canonical sampling resolutions, train/val/test splits, and standard forecasting horizons (96, 192, 336, 720).", "Periodicity parameters S can be established a priori from calendar metadata or computed using training-set power spectral density / autocorrelation analysis."], "uncertainties": [], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_811279__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_811279__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# Experimental Design Specification: Implementation, Training, and Evaluation of CycleNet for Periodic Time Series Forecasting\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary & Problem Scope\\n\\nPeriodic time series forecasting is critical across diverse domains\\u2014including power grid load forecasting, traffic flow optimization, and environmental monitoring. Conventional deep architectures (e.g., vanilla Transformers) often fail to maintain strict phase consistency across long horizons or incur excessive computational overhead, whereas simple linear models (e.g., DLinear) may lack explicit capacity to represent recurring intra-cycle dynamics and multi-harmonic patterns.\\n\\n**CycleNet** bridges this gap through a **linear-cycle hybrid architecture**. By coupling a learnable periodic cycle representation with linear projection backbones and trend-residual decoupling, CycleNet explicitly incorporates periodic priors while preserving computational efficiency ($O(L \\\\cdot H)$ linear parameter complexity per channel). This document provides an end-to-end, reproducible experimental design to implement, train, and comprehensively benchmark CycleNet against modern forecasting paradigms.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Mathematical Formulation of CycleNet\\n\\n### 2.1. Notation and Input/Output Spaces\\nLet $\\\\mathbf{X}_{1:L} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{L \\\\times C}$ denote the historical lookback window of length $L$ across $C$ variates, where $\\\\mathbf{x}_t \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^C$ is the observation vector at discrete time index $t \\\\in \\\\{1, \\\\dots, L\\\\}$. The objective is to forecast the future values $\\\\mathbf{Y}_{L+1:L+H} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{H \\\\times C}$ over a forecasting horizon $H$.\\n\\n### 2.2. Reversible Instance Normalization (RevIN)\\nTo handle non-stationary distribution shifts (mean drift and variance scaling across temporal windows), CycleNet applies Reversible Instance Normalization without affine parameters (or with learnable affine scaling):\\n\\n$$\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\mu}_X = \\\\frac{1}{L} \\\\sum_{t=1}^L \\\\mathbf{x}_t \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^C, \\\\quad \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\sigma}_X^2 = \\\\frac{1}{L} \\\\sum_{t=1}^L (\\\\mathbf{x}_t - \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\mu}_X)^2 + \\\\epsilon \\\\cdot \\\\mathbf{1} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^C$$\\n\\nThe normalized input $\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{L \\\\times C}$ is computed as:\\n$$\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{x}}_t = \\\\frac{\\\\mathbf{x}_t - \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\mu}_X}{\\\\boldsymbol{\\\\sigma}_X}, \\\\quad \\\\forall t \\\\in \\\\{1, \\\\dots, L\\\\}$$\\n\\nUpon generating the normalized prediction $\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{Y}} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{H \\\\times C}$, the final forecast is retrieved via the exact inverse operation:\\n$$\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf{y}}_{L+h} = \\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{y}}_{L+h} \\\\odot \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\sigma}_X + \\\\boldsymbol{\\\\mu}_X, \\\\quad \\\\forall h \\\\in \\\\{1, \\\\dots, H\\\\}$$\\n\\n### 2.3. Cycle Indexing and Learnable Cycle Representations\\n\\n#### Single Dominant Period\\nLet $S \\\\in \\\\mathbb{N}^+$ be the fundamental period length (e.g., $S = 24$ for hourly observations with daily seasonality). Every absolute time step $t$ corresponds to an intra-cycle phase index $p_t \\\\in \\\\{0, 1, \\\\dots, S-1\\\\}$:\\n$$p_t = t \\\\pmod S$$\\n\\nCycleNet instantiates a learnable cycle memory matrix $\\\\mathbf{C} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{S \\\\times D}$, where $D$ is the embedding dimension. For channel-independent architectures, $\\\\mathbf{C}$ can be shared across all variates or instantiated per channel $\\\\mathbf{C}^{(c)} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{S \\\\times D}$ ($c \\\\in \\\\{1, \\\\dots, C\\\\}$).\\n\\nFor the lookback window and the future horizon, the cycle features are sliced by phase:\\n$$\\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{in}} = \\\\left[ \\\\mathbf{C}[p_1], \\\\mathbf{C}[p_2], \\\\dots, \\\\mathbf{C}[p_L] \\\\right]^\\\\top \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{L \\\\times D}$$\\n$$\\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{out}} = \\\\left[ \\\\mathbf{C}[p_{L+1}], \\\\mathbf{C}[p_{L+2}], \\\\dots, \\\\mathbf{C}[p_{L+H}] \\\\right]^\\\\top \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{H \\\\times D}$$\\n\\n#### Multi-Periodic Extension (Harmonic Superposition)\\nReal-world systems frequently feature multiple overlapping seasonalities (e.g., daily cycle $S_1 = 24$ and weekly cycle $S_2 = 168$). CycleNet extends the learnable cycle representation via multi-harmonic additive superposition:\\n$$\\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{multi}}[t] = \\\\sum_{m=1}^M \\\\mathbf{W}_m \\\\mathbf{C}^{(m)}\\\\left[ t \\\\pmod{S_m} \\\\right]$$\\nwhere $M$ is the number of identified periodicities, $\\\\mathbf{C}^{(m)} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{S_m \\\\times D_m}$ is the cycle dictionary for the $m$-th frequency component, and $\\\\mathbf{W}_m \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{D \\\\times D_m}$ is a linear projection aligning channel capacities.\\n\\n### 2.4. Decoupled Architecture: Cycle & Trend/Residual Branches\\n\\nThe normalized input $\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}}$ is decomposed into a trend/slowly varying residual component and a cyclical component:\\n\\n1. **Trend/Residual Branch**:\\n   The trend/residual sequence captures long-term non-periodic evolutions. It is extracted either via a 1D average pooling kernel of size $k$ (moving average filter, $k \\\\approx S$) or directly modeled via a linear residual backbone:\\n   $$\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}}_{\\\\text{trend}} = \\\\text{AvgPool1D}(\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}}, \\\\text{kernel\\\\_size}=k, \\\\text{stride}=1, \\\\text{padding}=\\\\lfloor k/2 \\\\rfloor)$$\\n   $$\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}}_{\\\\text{res}} = \\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}} - \\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}}_{\\\\text{trend}}$$\\n   The trend forecast is mapped linearly across the temporal dimension:\\n   $$\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\\\text{trend}} = \\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{trend}} \\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}}_{\\\\text{trend}} + \\\\mathbf{b}_{\\\\text{trend}}, \\\\quad \\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{trend}} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{H \\\\times L}$$\\n\\n2. **Cycle Branch**:\\n   The cyclical dynamics are captured by combining historical cyclical deviations $\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}}_{\\\\text{res}}$ with the learnable future cycle prototypes $\\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{out}}$:\\n   $$\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\\\text{cycle}} = \\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{hist}} \\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}}_{\\\\text{res}} + \\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{proj}} \\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{out}}$$\\n   where $\\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{hist}} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{H \\\\times L}$ maps historical cyclical residuals into the forecast horizon, and $\\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{proj}} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{1 \\\\times D}$ projects the multidimensional future cycle embedding into univariate channel values (or $\\\\mathbb{R}^{C \\\\times D}$ for channel-dependent modeling).\\n\\n3. **Composite Aggregation**:\\n   The complete normalized forecast combines the outputs of both branches:\\n   $$\\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{Y}} = \\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\\\text{trend}} + \\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{Y}}_{\\\\text{cycle}}$$\\n   followed by the RevIN denormalization step to produce the final physical prediction $\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf{Y}}$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Implementation and Training Specification\\n\\n### 3.1. Loss Function Formulations\\n1. **Primary Forecasting Loss (Mean Squared Error - MSE)**:\\n   $$\\\\mathcal{L}_{\\\\text{MSE}} = \\\\frac{1}{H \\\\cdot C} \\\\sum_{h=1}^H \\\\sum_{c=1}^C \\\\left( Y_{L+h, c} - \\\\hat{Y}_{L+h, c} \\\\right)^2$$\\n\\n2. **Periodic Smoothness Regularization (Optional Auxiliary Loss)**:\\n   To prevent rapid, unphysical high-frequency fluctuations within the learnable cycle embedding table $\\\\mathbf{C} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{S \\\\times D}$, a circular Dirichlet / Total Variation penalty is imposed:\\n   $$\\\\mathcal{L}_{\\\\text{reg}}(\\\\mathbf{C}) = \\\\frac{1}{S} \\\\sum_{s=0}^{S-1} \\\\left\\\\| \\\\mathbf{C}[(s+1) \\\\pmod S] - \\\\mathbf{C}[s] \\\\right\\\\|_2^2$$\\n\\n3. **Composite Objective**:\\n   $$\\\\mathcal{L}_{\\\\text{total}} = \\\\mathcal{L}_{\\\\text{MSE}} + \\\\lambda_{\\\\text{reg}} \\\\mathcal{L}_{\\\\text{reg}}(\\\\mathbf{C}), \\\\quad \\\\text{with } \\\\lambda_{\\\\text{reg}} \\\\in \\\\{0, 10^{-4}, 10^{-3}\\\\}$$\\n\\n### 3.2. Optimization Setup & Hyperparameters\\n- **Optimizer**: AdamW ($\\\\beta_1 = 0.9, \\\\beta_2 = 0.999$, weight decay $= 10^{-4}$).\\n- **Learning Rate Schedule**: Cosine Annealing learning rate schedule with an initial learning rate $\\\\eta_{\\\\text{init}} = 10^{-3}$ and minimum learning rate $\\\\eta_{\\\\text{min}} = 10^{-6}$ over $E = 50$ epochs, preceded by a linear warm-up phase of 5 epochs.\\n- **Batch Size**: 32 for multivariate benchmarks ($C > 20$); 64 or 128 for lower-dimensional series ($C \\\\le 7$).\\n- **Early Stopping**: Patience of 10 epochs monitoring validation MSE loss; best model weights are checkpointed and reloaded for testing.\\n- **Gradient Clipping**: Maximal $\\\\ell_2$-norm threshold of $1.0$ to ensure numerical stability during initial training iterations.\\n\\n### 3.3. Channel Strategy: Channel Independence (CI) vs. Channel Mixing (CM)\\n- CycleNet adopts a **Channel-Independent (CI)** paradigm by default: weights $(\\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{trend}}, \\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{hist}})$ are shared across all $C$ channels while the cycle representation $\\\\mathbf{C}$ can be shared globally across channels to induce strong inductive bias and minimize parameter footprint.\\n- For high-dimensional datasets ($C > 100$), CI prevents overfitting and drastically reduces training latency.\\n\\n### 3.4. Computational Complexity and Memory Footprint\\n- **Time Complexity per Batch**:\\n  - RevIN: $O(L \\\\cdot C)$\\n  - Moving Average Decomposition: $O(L \\\\cdot C)$\\n  - Linear Projections: $O(L \\\\cdot H \\\\cdot C)$\\n  - Cycle Lookup and Projection: $O(H \\\\cdot D \\\\cdot C)$\\n  - Total time complexity is strictly linear in input/output lengths: $\\\\mathcal{O}\\\\left(C \\\\cdot (L \\\\cdot H + H \\\\cdot D)\\\\right)$, avoiding the quadratic $\\\\mathcal{O}(L^2)$ attention bottleneck of standard Transformers.\\n- **Parameter Footprint**:\\n  - Total parameters: $P \\\\approx 2 \\\\cdot (L \\\\times H) + (S \\\\times D) + (D \\\\times 1)$.\\n  - For $L=96, H=96, S=24, D=16$: $P \\\\approx 2(9216) + 384 + 16 \\\\approx 18.8\\\\text{k}$ parameters. This allows real-time CPU deployment and ultra-fast training iterations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Experimental Setup, Datasets, and Evaluation Controls\\n\\n### 4.1. Benchmark Datasets\\n\\n#### 1. Real-World Benchmarks\\nAll datasets are partitioned chronologically (70% Train, 10% Validation, 20% Test) with strict avoidance of lookahead leakage:\\n- **Electricity Load (ECL)**: Hourly electricity consumption of 321 clients. Fundamental cycle $S = 24$ (daily) and $S = 168$ (weekly).\\n- **Traffic (PeMS)**: Hourly road occupancy rates from California Department of Transportation (862 sensors). Strong daily commute cycle ($S = 24$).\\n- **Weather**: Local climatological data recorded at 10-minute intervals (21 variables). Diurnal cycle $S = 144$ (steps per 24 hours).\\n- **ETT (Electricity Transformer Temperature)**:\\n  - Hourly series: $\\\\text{ETTh1}, \\\\text{ETTh2}$ ($S = 24$).\\n  - 15-minute series: $\\\\text{ETTm1}, \\\\text{ETTm2}$ ($S = 96$).\\n\\n#### 2. Synthetic Benchmark (Controlled Periodic Stress-Testing)\\nTo evaluate CycleNet under isolated, controllable conditions, we define a synthetic data generation process with known ground-truth periodicity:\\n$$x_t = \\\\sum_{m=1}^2 A_m \\\\sin\\\\left(\\\\frac{2\\\\pi t}{S_m} + \\\\phi_m\\\\right) + \\\\beta \\\\cdot t + \\\\gamma_t + \\\\epsilon_t$$\\nwhere:\\n- Primary periods: $S_1 = 24$ (daily), $S_2 = 168$ (weekly).\\n- $\\\\beta \\\\cdot t$: Linear trend component ($\\\\beta \\\\in \\\\{0.0, 0.05\\\\}$).\\n- $\\\\gamma_t$: Abrupt regime-shift mean jump at $t = T/2$.\\n- $\\\\epsilon_t \\\\sim \\\\mathcal{N}(0, \\\\sigma^2)$: Gaussian noise with controlled signal-to-noise ratios (SNR $\\\\in \\\\{5\\\\text{dB}, 15\\\\text{dB}, 30\\\\text{dB}\\\\}$).\\n\\n### 4.2. Baseline Models\\nCycleNet is evaluated against competitive models spanning diverse design paradigms:\\n1. **Linear/Decomposition Baselines**:\\n   - **DLinear**: Direct linear decomposition mapping seasonal and trend components.\\n   - **NLinear**: Linear model with normalization subtraction to handle non-stationarity.\\n   - **TiDE**: Time-series Dense Encoder-decoder incorporating static and dynamic covariates.\\n2. **2D & Multi-Periodic Neural Models**:\\n   - **TimesNet**: Transforms 1D series into 2D variations based on dominant FFT frequencies and applies 2D convolutions.\\n3. **Patch-based & Attention Transformers**:\\n   - **PatchTST**: State-of-the-art patch-based Transformer with channel independence.\\n   - **Autoformer**: Auto-correlation mechanism with built-in seasonal-trend decomposition.\\n\\n### 4.3. Evaluation Controls & Experimental Protocol\\n- **Fixed Lookback Window**: Standardized input length $L = 96$ (and $L = 336$ for long-history analysis).\\n- **Forecasting Horizons**: $H \\\\in \\\\{96, 192, 336, 720\\\\}$ steps across all datasets.\\n- **Statistical Significance**: Each experimental run is executed across **3 independent random initialization seeds** (e.g., seeds `[2024, 2025, 2026]`). Results are reported as mean $\\\\pm$ standard error.\\n- **Evaluation Metrics**:\\n  - **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**: $\\\\frac{1}{N H C} \\\\sum_{i=1}^N \\\\sum_{h=1}^H \\\\sum_{c=1}^C (Y_{h, c}^{(i)} - \\\\hat{Y}_{h, c}^{(i)})^2$\\n  - **Mean Absolute Error (MAE)**: $\\\\frac{1}{N H C} \\\\sum_{i=1}^N \\\\sum_{h=1}^H \\\\sum_{c=1}^C |Y_{h, c}^{(i)} - \\\\hat{Y}_{h, c}^{(i)}|$\\n  - **Computational Efficiency**: Number of trainable parameters, Multiply-Accumulate operations (MACs), and inference latency (milliseconds per sample on CPU and GPU).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Comprehensive Analysis and Ablation Plan\\n\\nTo isolate the source of predictive gains and test robustness, the experiment design incorporates five structured ablation experiments:\\n\\n### Ablation 1: Efficacy of Learnable Cycle Representations\\n- **Hypothesis**: Learnable cycle embeddings $\\\\mathbf{C} \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{S \\\\times D}$ outperform static mathematical encodings by adapting to asymmetric, non-sinusoidal waveforms.\\n- **Configurations Compared**:\\n  1. *CycleNet (Full)*: Learnable cycle embedding dictionary $\\\\mathbf{C}$.\\n  2. *CycleNet-Fourier*: Fixed sinusoidal harmonics: $\\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{fixed}}[t, 2k] = \\\\sin(2\\\\pi k t / S)$, $\\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{fixed}}[t, 2k+1] = \\\\cos(2\\\\pi k t / S)$.\\n  3. *CycleNet-ZeroCycle*: $\\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{out}}$ disabled (reduces to a standard DLinear/linear mapping).\\n  4. *CycleNet-OneHot*: Static one-hot phase encoding.\\n\\n### Ablation 2: Robustness to Period Misspecification & Phase Jitter\\n- **Hypothesis**: In real-world data, the exact period may exhibit drift or measurement inaccuracies.\\n- **Test Protocol**: On the synthetic dataset and Electricity dataset:\\n  - Perturb the specified period $S_{\\\\text{spec}} \\\\in \\\\{S - 2, S - 1, S, S + 1, S + 2\\\\}$.\\n  - Inject random phase shifts / timestamp jitter $\\\\Delta t \\\\sim \\\\text{Bernoulli}(p)$ to assess CycleNet's degradation curve under imperfect temporal alignment.\\n\\n### Ablation 3: Decomposition Mechanics (Cycle + Trend vs. Monolithic)\\n- **Hypothesis**: Explicit separation of trend/residuals prevents non-periodic drifts from corrupting the periodic representation.\\n- **Configurations Compared**:\\n  1. *Dual-branch CycleNet* (Trend + Cycle branches).\\n  2. *Single-branch CycleNet* (Direct mapping: $\\\\hat{\\\\mathbf{Y}} = \\\\mathbf{W} \\\\tilde{\\\\mathbf{X}} + \\\\mathbf{W}_c \\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{out}}$ without residual decoupling).\\n  3. *Cycle-only*: Omitting the trend branch entirely.\\n\\n### Ablation 4: Multi-Periodic Superposition Analysis\\n- **Hypothesis**: Modeling compound cycles (e.g., daily + weekly) simultaneously yields lower long-term error ($H = 720$) than relying on a single base period.\\n- **Configurations Compared**:\\n  - Single cycle: $S_1 = 24$ (diurnal only).\\n  - Single cycle: $S_2 = 168$ (weekly only).\\n  - Harmonic Superposition: Multi-harmonic additive cycle modeling ($S_1 = 24 + S_2 = 168$).\\n\\n### Ablation 5: Cycle Representation Capacity Scaling\\n- **Hypothesis**: There is an optimal latent dimension $D$ balancing expressive capacity against parameter redundancy.\\n- **Test Grid**: Sweep embedding dimension $D \\\\in \\\\{1, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64\\\\}$. Evaluate both predictive accuracy (MSE) and parameter efficiency.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Feasibility, Practical Deployment, and Reproducibility\\n\\n### 6.1. Deployment Considerations\\n1. **Low-Latency Edge Inference**:\\n   Because the cycle representation $\\\\mathbf{C}_{\\\\text{out}}$ for future horizons depends solely on the time index $t \\\\pmod S$, it can be pre-computed and stored as a static lookup table in edge deployments. The remaining operations are single matrix multiplications and vector additions, enabling sub-millisecond inference on modest CPU architectures.\\n2. **Handling Missing Observations & Phase Alignment**:\\n   In streaming environments with missing data points, naive index increments can cause phase shift errors. The deployment pipeline must rely on calendar-based absolute timestamps (modulo $S$ over elapsed time) rather than sequential sample counts, ensuring phase synchronization is invariant to missing observations.\\n3. **Cold-Start Adaptation**:\\n   When deploying to new time series channels with minimal history, the shared cycle table $\\\\mathbf{C}$ serves as a strong global periodic prior, stabilizing predictions even when historical lookback windows are noisy.\\n\\n### 6.2. Sanity Checks and Dry-Run Assertions\\nTo guarantee bug-free implementation before executing full benchmarks, the following programmatic sanity assertions should be embedded in the test pipeline:\\n- **Phase Invariance**: Verifying that a circular roll of input and timestamp indices by exactly $k \\\\cdot S$ produces an identically rolled forecast.\\n- **Reversible Normalization Invertibility**: Asserting $\\\\|\\\\text{RevIN}^{-1}(\\\\text{RevIN}(\\\\mathbf{X})) - \\\\mathbf{X}\\\\|_\\\\infty < 10^{-6}$.\\n- **Gradient Flow Check**: Ensuring non-zero gradients flow to both the linear trend weights $\\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{trend}}$ and the cycle table $\\\\mathbf{C}$ after the backward pass:\\n  $$\\\\min(\\\\|\\\\nabla_{\\\\mathbf{W}_{\\\\text{trend}}} \\\\mathcal{L}\\\\|_2, \\\\|\\\\nabla_{\\\\mathbf{C}} \\\\mathcal{L}\\\\|_2) > 0$$\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Summary of Complete Experimental Workflow\\n\\n```\\n[Time Series Data] \\n       \\u2502\\n       \\u25bc\\n[Data Preprocessing & Chronological Splitting (70/10/20)]\\n       \\u2502\\n       \\u25bc\\n[Periodicity Identification (FFT / Sampling Rate S1, S2)]\\n       \\u2502\\n       \\u25bc\\n[CycleNet Architecture]\\n  \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 RevIN Normalization\\n  \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Trend/Residual Decomposition (Moving Average)\\n  \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Dual Branch Processing:\\n  \\u2502     \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Trend Branch: W_trend * X_trend\\n  \\u2502     \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 Cycle Branch: W_hist * X_res + W_proj * C_out(t mod S)\\n  \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Composite Sum: Y_norm = Y_trend + Y_cycle\\n  \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 RevIN Denormalization -> Final Predictions\\n       \\u2502\\n       \\u25bc\\n[Optimization (AdamW, Cosine Annealing, Early Stopping on Val MSE)]\\n       \\u2502\\n       \\u25bc\\n[Evaluation against Baselines (DLinear, PatchTST, TimesNet, TiDE)]\\n       \\u2502\\n       \\u25bc\\n[Ablations: Learnable vs. Static Cycles, S Misspecification, Capacity D]\\n```\\n\\nThis experimental design provides a self-contained, mathematically explicit, and methodologically sound specification to implement, train, and validate CycleNet for periodic time series forecasting without relying on unverified assumptions or fabricated outcomes.\", \"summary\": \"Deliver complete experimental design specification for CycleNet periodic time series forecasting\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"uncertainties\": [], \"evidence\": \"Completed exploratory checks on the container environment (/workspace, Python sys.path, and pip list) confirming that no pre-existing code or dependencies are present, and validated that the design adheres to Option A (linear-cycle hybrid with multi-periodic harmonic extensions) as instructed and approved by the researcher.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Task requirements dictate a design-only specification without physical model execution or fabrication of empirical numerical results.\", \"The standard public time series benchmarks (Electricity, Traffic, Weather, ETT) follow their canonical sampling resolutions, train/val/test splits, and standard forecasting horizons (96, 192, 336, 720).\", \"Periodicity parameters S can be established a priori from calendar metadata or computed using training-set power spectral density / autocorrelation analysis.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "OmbUaqg7t47j9Q_0wv24CQ", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
